---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { PHONE, PHONE_TEL } from '../../lib/site.ts';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="What you get"
  description="Independent floor elevation survey, on-site walkthrough, written guidance, and questions to ask repair contractors — Arizona Foundation Repair Advocate."
>
  <div class="container">
    <h1 class="page-title">What you get</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      A paid decision tool: documentation and an independent read before you commit to a high-cost
      repair — not a path to our pier contract (we don't sell one).
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2>What you receive</h2>
      <p>For the fee, you walk away with:</p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>
          Repeatable floor elevation survey of accessible living levels (slab-on-grade and main
          floors), typically with a Pro Level water manometer — widely accepted for its
          repeatability and preferred by many geotechnical firms
        </li>
        <li>Topographic contour map showing high and low areas</li>
        <li>On-site walkthrough — cracks, doors, drainage, context</li>
        <li>
          Plain-language discussion of settlement vs heave patterns and what the numbers suggest for
          next steps
        </li>
        <li>Written summary and 12-month watch list</li>
        <li>Questions to ask foundation repair contractors when they come out</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Monitor &amp; resurvey (when it fits)</h2>
      <p>
        When the evidence supports watching instead of repairing now, monitoring is a defined
        product: a documented baseline, what to watch for, and an optional return visit to compare.
        It is not a consolation prize — it is the right tool when urgency is not supported.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Second opinion add-on</h2>
      <p>
        If you already have another company's floor survey, estimate, or engineer's letter, bring
        it. We'll read pier count, methods, and pricing against what we measure — line by line when
        it matters.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          A floor survey from a repair company isn't wrong because of the instrument. The instrument
          can be fine; the visit still sits inside a repair business. You are paying for independence
          — so the interpretation is not tied to selling the next step.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What we don't do</h2>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>Sell helical piers, push piers, foam, or any repair package</li>
        <li>Offer "free" inspections that convert to a sales visit</li>
        <li>Provide stamped Arizona engineering reports (we'll tell you when you need a PE)</li>
        <li>Direct means and methods during someone else's repair (we can advise you and ask questions)</li>
        <li>Crawl every crawlspace — limited crawlspace work by design; slabs and accessible floors are our focus</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Typical investment</h2>
      <p>
        Independent floor-level surveys in northern and central Arizona typically run
        <strong>$1,000–$1,250</strong> depending on size and travel. You'll know the fee before we
        schedule. No surprise upsells.
      </p>
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href={`tel:${PHONE_TEL}`} style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">Call to schedule — {PHONE}</a>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
